Evaluate at $r=0$:
$h'(0) = (x+1) \ln(x+1) \cdot 1 - x \ln x \cdot 1 = (x+1)\ln(x+1) - x \ln x$.
So $\ln g(x) = (x+1)\ln(x+1) - x \ln x$.
Therefore, $g(x) = e^{(x+1)\ln(x+1) - x \ln x}$.
Simplify the exponent:
$(x+1)\ln(x+1) - x \ln x = x \ln(x+1) + \ln(x+1) - x \ln x$
$= x (\ln(x+1) - \ln x) + \ln(x+1)$
$= x \ln(\frac{x+1}{x}) + \ln(x+1)$
$= x \ln(1 + \frac{1}{x}) + \ln(x+1)$.

We want to calculate $\lim_{x \to \infty} \frac{g(x)}{x}$.
