So $\frac{1}{u} \ln(1+u) \approx 1 \cdot u / u = 1$. Actually it goes to 1.
Specifically, $\ln(1+u) = u - u^2/2 + O(u^3)$.
So $\frac{1}{u} \ln(1+u) = 1 - u/2 + O(u^2) \to 1$.
Thus $e^{x \ln(1 + \frac{1}{x})} \to e^1 = e$.
Therefore, $L = 1 \cdot e = e$.
